<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sultan bin Saqr Al Qasimi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Al Qasimi</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> bin Saqr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Al Qasimi</a> was sheikh of the Qawasim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a> and Sharjah for most of the period from 1803 to 1866. After British attacks on Ras al-Khaimah in 1809 and 1819 he made Sharjah the family's chief seat, and he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-maritime-treaty-of-1820">General Maritime Treaty of 1820</a> and the later truces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> bin Saqr became sheikh while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Qawasim</a> were at war with the Sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, who claimed the coastal towns of the southern Gulf. A treaty with the British signed on 6 February 1806 bound the Qawasim to respect ships under the British flag in return for access to Indian ports. In May 1808 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khor-fakkan">Khor Fakkan</a> from Muscat, but the Saudi Amir then removed him, held him prisoner at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> and put Hassan bin Rahma Al Qasimi in his place.</p><p>He escaped by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and Mokha to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, where Saiyid Said received him. An expedition from Muscat in 1813 failed to restore him, but a second the next year installed him as ruler of Sharjah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-lengeh">Lingeh</a>. After the British sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a> in 1819 and removed Hassan bin Rahma, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> signed the General Maritime Treaty on 4 February 1820 at Falayah Fort as Sheikh of Sharjah and Ras Al Khaimah, built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharjah-fort">Sharjah Fort</a> the same year and later rebuilt Ras al-Khaimah from the rubble of the old town.</p><p>He ruled through relatives placed as walis. By 1824 he had a client in Umm Al Quwain and the acknowledgement of Ajman, and in 1825 he married into Dubai's ruling family. War with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bani-yas">Bani Yas</a> of Abu Dhabi followed: on 10 September 1833 his landing near Abu Dhabi was surrounded and routed, but a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> from November 1833 brought a settlement in 1834 under which the people of Dubai were to be subjects of Sharjah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The British pearling-season truces that began in 1835 led to the Perpetual Maritime Truce of May 1853, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> signed as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Al Qasimi</a>. In 1850 he reached a compromise with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> setting a line between Sharjah and Khor Kalba, ending almost a century of intermittent conflict. In May 1855 he besieged rebellious Hamriyah with some 3,000 men from Ajman; his son Abdullah was killed, and the British captain Kemball negotiated a peace instead of enforcing Sultan's rule.</p><p>In his last years he took no active part in the quarrels among his kin over Sharjah, which his son Khalid ended in 1860 by killing his rival Mohammed. Sultan died in 1866 at the age of 85 and was succeeded by Khalid bin Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Al Qasimi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sultan bin Saqr Al Qasimi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive figure in Sharjah’s rise was Sheikh <b>Sultan bin Saqr Al Qasimi</b>, who became head of the Qawasim in 1803 and ruled, with interruptions, until his death in 1866.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sheikhdom-of-sharjah">Sheikhdom of Sharjah · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
